page guide_usb {
  title = "Host USB devices"
  chapter_header "Host USB devices" {
    kicker       = "The guide · explanation"
    reading_time = "6 min read"
    updated      = "2026-10-19"
    version      = "vmlab 0.9"
  }

  p <<'DOC'
A running VM can be handed a USB device plugged into the host — a flash
drive, a hardware token, a serial adapter, a mouse — and given it back, with
no restart. While the VM holds the device the host does not have it: its host
driver lets go, and the guest sees the device as if it had just been plugged
in. This chapter covers the controller a VM needs, how you name a device, the
refusals an attach can meet, and what happens on unplug, stop and snapshot.
The verbs are in [vmlab usb](cmd_usb).
DOC

  h2 "Give the VM a controller"

  p <<'DOC'
A device hot-plugs onto a USB 3 (xHCI) controller that the VM gets only when
it starts with `usb = true`. Set it on the `vm` block, or on a
[profile](guide_profiles) to give every VM of that profile one; a template
has no say in it. It is off by default.
DOC

  code wcl { filename = "vmlab.wcl" source = <<'SRC'
import <vmlab.wcl>

lab "usbtest" {
  vm "u1" {
    template = "x86_64/alpine-3.23"
    usb      = true
  }
}
SRC
  }

  p <<'DOC'
The flag is opt-in because it changes the VM's hardware. An online snapshot
taken without the controller does not restore once it is there, and on `q35`
the absolute-pointer tablet moves onto the new controller. On `virt`
(aarch64, riscv64), which already has an xHCI, nothing is added: the existing
controller is the one devices attach to. A container has no USB bus and
refuses every attach.
DOC

  h2 "Find the device"

  p <<'DOC'
`vmlab usb list` prints every device the host has, hubs hidden, with the VM
holding each. It works from any directory: holders are collected from every
running lab.
DOC

  code text { source = <<'TXT'
PORT         ID         SERIAL  SPEED  ATTACHED TO  DEVICE
1-2.1.4.1.4  046d:c547  -       full   -            Logitech USB Receiver
1-3          0781:5581  BB22    super  -            SanDisk Ultra
TXT
  }

  p <<'DOC'
Name a device by `vid:pid` (`0781:5581`), by `vid:pid@serial` when two
identical devices are plugged in (`0781:5581@BB22`), or by the socket it is
in (`port:1-3`), which always names exactly one. A name that matches several
devices is refused with each candidate's `port:` selector, so you can pick.
DOC

  h2 "Attach and detach"

  code sh { source = <<'SH'
vmlab usb attach u1 0781:5581@BB22
vmlab exec u1 -- lsusb
vmlab usb detach u1 0781:5581@BB22
SH
  }

  p <<'DOC'
An attach checks, in order: the VM is running and has the controller; the
selector names one device; no other VM, in any lab, holds it; the device is
not a keyboard or mouse; and vmlab can open its device node. Two of these
need a word.
DOC

  p <<'DOC'
**Keyboards and mice.** A device with a HID boot keyboard or mouse interface
is refused unless you pass `--force`, because the host is probably using it
and loses it while the VM has it. A wireless receiver often carries both
interfaces and is named a keyboard. Do not attach the host's only keyboard:
with it gone there is nothing left to type the detach with.
DOC

  p <<'DOC'
**The device node.** QEMU opens `/dev/bus/usb/BBB/DDD` as you, and a stock
node is not writable by your user. The first attach of a device usually
stops here, naming the node, a `setfacl` command that grants access until the
device is replugged, and a udev rule that grants it for good. See
[Troubleshooting](apx_troubleshooting).
DOC

  p <<'DOC'
`vmlab usb detach <vm> --all` gives back everything a VM holds, and stopping
the VM does the same. Nothing about an attachment is saved: a VM that starts
again starts with no host devices.
DOC

  h2 "Unplugging, snapshots and events"

  p <<'DOC'
An attachment is pinned to one plug-in of the device. Pull it out of the host
and vmlab notices within about two seconds and drops it from the VM; plug it
back in and it returns to the host, not to the VM. A different device plugged
into the same socket is never handed to the guest.
DOC

  p <<'DOC'
An online snapshot of a VM holding any host device is refused until you
detach them, and an online restore detaches them first; see
[Snapshots](guide_snapshots). Each attach and detach emits `usb.attached` or
`usb.detached` (see [Events](ref_events)); a detach vmlab made on its own
carries a `reason`, `unplugged` or `restore`.
DOC

  h2 "From a script"

  p <<'DOC'
The same operations exist in wscript: `lab.usb_devices()` lists the host's
devices, and a machine handle has `usb_attach`, `usb_attach_force`,
`usb_detach`, `usb_detach_all` and `usb_attached`. They are part of wscript
surface 3. See [wscript API: Machine](ref_ws_machine).
DOC

  code ws { filename = "usb.ws" source = <<'SRC'
use vmlab

fn main(lab: Lab) {
    let Ok(vm) = lab.machine("u1") else { return }
    let dev = vm.usb_attach("0781:5581@BB22").expect("attach")
    lab.log("attached " + dev.id)
    vm.exec("sh", ["-c", "lsusb"]).expect("lsusb")
    vm.usb_detach(dev.id).expect("detach")
}
SRC
  }
}
